            "paragraphs": [
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "1 \u00b7 Cost of Equity (CAPM) \u2014 from scratch",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "13pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Start with the question: what return makes an investor hand over cash? At minimum, what a riskless government bond pays (rf) \u2014 otherwise they just buy the bond. Anything riskier must pay MORE.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "How much more? Investors hold many stocks, so the only risk they cannot diversify away is co-movement with the whole market. \u03b2 measures that: \u03b2 = 1 moves with the market, \u03b2 = 1.2 swings 20% harder. The market's own reward for risk is the equity risk premium, ERP = Rm \u2212 rf.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "So the demanded return builds itself:  Ke = rf + \u03b2 \u00d7 ERP = 4.53 + 1.20 \u00d7 4.50 = 9.93%.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "\u2192 tab 3 \u00b7 Market & Cost of Capital",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
